![security-high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/security-high-priority.svg) ![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

Since `GainCurve` has public fields and derives `Deserialize` (when the `serde` feature is enabled), it is possible to construct or deserialize a `GainCurve` with invalid or non-finite `input_range` values (e.g., `input_range.0 >= input_range.1` or containing `NaN`). If this occurs, calling `level.clamp(self.input_range.0, self.input_range.1)` will panic because `clamp` requires `min <= max` and finite bounds. Adding a robust guard to check these conditions and return a default scale of `1.0` prevents unexpected panics in production.

```suggestion
    pub fn evaluate(&self, level: f32) -> f32 {
        if !level.is_finite()
            || !self.input_range.0.is_finite()
            || !self.input_range.1.is_finite()
            || self.input_range.0 >= self.input_range.1
        {
            return 1.0;
        }
```

